# tools/al/ales_stereo_taps_witness.rish — stereo_taps: the multi-tap delay carried into stereo, the same tap set on both channels, each channel read off its own frozen dry snapshot — the second rung of the stereo time-based wing, the echo's snapshot sibling (ALES183).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES183.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-090818_fill-ales183-lotus-stereo-multi-tap.md
# Stands on: lotus/taps.rye (ALES68 — the mono multi-tap run once per channel, its TapError reused whole and its precheck (newly factored out) reused for the up-front atomic validation), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye
# (ALES2 — the Clip and the one true saturate the mono multi-tap reuses).
#
# lotus/stereo_taps.rye multi-taps a stereo master: it runs ALES68's proven mono multitap on BOTH channels with the SAME tap set — each sample the dry input plus the sum of every tap's attenuated copy of the dry sample its delay earlier, y[i] =
# saturate(x[i] + sum over k of num_k over den_k times x[i-d_k]). ALES181 opened the stereo time-based wing with the feedback echo (each tap reads the live output, a decaying train); the multi-tap is the wing's other primitive — several fixed taps
# of the dry standing at their own chosen levels, the discrete early-reflection pattern a single feeding echo cannot voice. Like the stereo vibrato and chorus and unlike the feedback echo, it carries a snapshot read into stereo, and the snapshot is
# what lets the panned integer ratio survive exactly. The same tap set on both channels, each read off its own dry snapshot, is the linked stereo multi-tap, both early-reflection patterns landing in lockstep. A tap's delay is a sample count and its
# level a named fraction the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same tap set on each channel preserves the image for free; mono multitap snapshots the dry prefix of its own clip, so run once per channel the left reads the
# left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed — no cross-channel snapshot. Because every tap reads the frozen dry and the sum is linear in the input away from saturation, an identical-channel master stays identical AND a panned pair kept off
# the rail keeps its exact ratio — the stronger both-cases image law the snapshot rungs earn and the feedback echo cannot. The whole sum saturates once per channel; unlike the feedback echo a tap at unity is legal (no loop to run away). It validates
# the tap set and span once against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted (BadTapCount, BadDelay, BadLevel, BadRange), so a refusal never taps one channel and leaves the other torn; TapError reused whole. Values only, never a
# length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is three laws: the stereo multi-tap law (each channel equal to ALES68's mono multitap with the same tap set byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing), the dry-identity law (a zero-level tap
# set the dry master on both), and the image / balance / silence / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_taps_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-taps: Language EN — stereo_taps multi-taps a stereo master by running ALES68's proven mono multitap on both channels with the same tap set: each sample the dry input plus the sum of every tap's attenuated copy of the dry sample its delay earlier. ALES181 opened the stereo time-based wing with the feedback echo, where each tap reads the live output and the train decays; the multi-tap is the wing's other primitive, several fixed taps of the dry standing at their own chosen levels, the discrete early-reflection pattern a single feeding echo cannot voice. Like the stereo vibrato and chorus and unlike the feedback echo, it carries a snapshot read into stereo, and the snapshot is what lets the panned integer ratio survive exactly. The same tap set on both channels, each read off its own dry snapshot, is the linked stereo multi-tap, both early-reflection patterns landing in lockstep. A tap's delay is a sample count and its level a named fraction the caller names, so the same tap set on each channel preserves the image for free; mono multitap snapshots the dry prefix of its own clip, so run once per channel the left reads the left's dry and the right the right's, never crossed, no cross-channel snapshot. Because every tap reads the frozen dry and the sum is linear in the input away from saturation, an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned pair kept off the rail keeps its exact ratio. The whole sum saturates once per channel; unlike the feedback echo a tap at unity is legal because there is no loop to run away. It validates the tap set and span once against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted, so a refusal never taps one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-taps: Style Radiant — the multi-tap is the discrete early-reflection craft, the sound a snare makes hitting three walls at three distances, and it is the delay line read the one way a feedback loop cannot give: several fixed taps of the dry, each standing at its own chosen level, none feeding the next. ALES181 seated the feedback echo as the wing's first primitive; this rung seats its snapshot sibling, run once per channel with the same tap set so both reflection patterns land in lockstep and the image is held. Each channel snapshots and reads its own dry, the left through the left's and the right through the right's, never crossed, so there is no cross-channel snapshot; and because every tap reads the frozen dry and the sum is linear in the input away from saturation, an identical-channel centred master stays identical and a panned pair kept off the rail keeps its exact ratio, a right exactly half the left staying exactly half after. This is the stronger both-cases image law the snapshot rungs earn and the feedback echo cannot, since the echo's feedback truncation drifts a panned ratio by ones. The whole sum, dry plus every tap, saturates once per channel, so a constructive pile-up pins to the rail rather than wrapping; and unlike the feedback echo a tap at unity is legal, a full-level discrete echo, because a multi-tap has no loop to run away. The crux is three laws: the stereo multi-tap law, each channel equal to ALES68's mono multitap with the same tap set byte for byte with the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed; the dry-identity law, a zero-level tap set equal to the dry master on both channels so the levels genuinely govern the reflections; and the image, balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law, an identical-channel master staying identical and a panned pair keeping its exact ratio, an all-silent master tapping to silence on both with the lengths held, a bad tap count, a bad delay, a bad level, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, and count zero the identity on both."
say "ales-stereo-taps: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 clips (left, right), each rung through ALES68's own bounded snapshot multi-tap, one frozen dry snapshot and at most max_taps multiply-divides per sample run in i64 and summed once, the fed sum saturating once per channel; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a multi-tap writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the tap set and span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is snapshotted (ALES68's precheck, factored out of the mono multitap and shared, mirroring the ALES71, ALES72, and ALES73 refactors), so a refusal never taps one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoTapError is ALES68's TapError (BadTapCount, BadDelay, BadLevel, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault. The stereo multi-tap law is proven by running both stereo_multitap and ALES68's mono multitap on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the dry-identity law proves a zero-level tap set equals the dry master on both; and the image, balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an identical-channel centred master staying identical and a panned pair kept off the rail keeping its exact ratio (the linear snapshot sum scaling, so the pan survives with no feedback truncation), an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the delay a count of sample indices rather than milliseconds and the level a named fraction at or below unity rather than a decibel, no cross-channel snapshot, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_taps"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_taps.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-taps: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_taps.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-taps: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-taps: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-taps' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-taps: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-taps line"
